    "output_text": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Set, Dict, Optional\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass TopologicalSortResult:\n    \"\"\"Result of a topological sort, including the order and any cycle information.\"\"\"\n    order: List[int]\n    has_cycle: bool\n    cycle: Optional[List[int]] = None\n\n\ndef topological_sort(\n    num_nodes: int,\n    edges: List[tuple]\n) -> TopologicalSortResult:\n    \"\"\"\n    Perform topological sorting on a directed acyclic graph.\n    \n    Args:\n        num_nodes: The number of nodes in the graph (labeled 0 to num_nodes-1).\n        edges: A list of (u, v) tuples representing a directed edge from u to v.\n        \n    Returns:\n        A TopologicalSortResult containing:\n        - order: A valid topological ordering of the nodes (if no cycle)\n        - has_cycle: True if the graph contains a cycle\n        - cycle: The cycle (if one is found), as a list of nodes\n    \"\"\"\n    # Build the adjacency list\n    adj: Dict[int, List[int]] = {i: [] for i in range(num_nodes)}\n    in_degree: Dict[int, int] = {i: 0 for i in range(num_nodes)}\n    \n    for u, v in edges:\n        if 0 <= u < num_nodes and 0 <= v < num_nodes:\n            adj[u].append(v)\n            in_degree[v] += 1\n    \n    # Use a stack (or queue) for nodes with in-degree 0\n    # We use a list as a stack for a DFS-based approach, but let's use\n    # the in-degree (Kahn's) approach for cycle detection and topological sort.\n    \n    # Actually, let's use a DFS-based approach for better cycle detection.\n    # We'll use the standard 3-color (white, gray, black) approach.\n    \n    # 0 = unvisited, 1 = in current path (gray), 2 = fully processed (black)\n    color: Dict[int, int] = {i: 0 for i in range(num_nodes)}\n    order: List[int] = []\n    has_cycle: bool = False\n    cycle: Optional[List[int]] = None\n    in_stack: List[int] = []\n",
